{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head c0c70300381730f3952db3c0a837c2beeb04121c, citing all five artifacts and naming the cmd/gitseq-mcp one.\n\nI treated the addressing as the security surface rather than as a feature, and tried to break the things that would matter if they broke.\n\nLegacy compatibility holds, and I probed it rather than reading it. The precheck is implemented twice, in Go and TypeScript, which is exactly where two implementations of one rule usually drift — so I compared the boundary sets character by character first: before is space plus ([{\u003c,;:!? in both, after is space plus )]}\u003e.,;:!? in both. Then I exercised the Go side against fifteen cases in a scratch worktree: user@example.com, hugh@example.org mid-sentence, /tmp/@claude/x, docs/@claude.md, a@claude and v1.2@claude are all correctly not mentions; @claude, (@claude), [@claude], @claude., \"hey @claude,\" the quoted form and a mention after a newline all are. @claude@example.com is not a mention, which is the case I most expected to slip through.\n\nRecipient integrity holds at every step I could find to attack. A session may only publish as the actor it is leased to — entry.fingerprint must equal the fingerprint of the signing key, so an author cannot be forged from a session that is not theirs. Recipients must be full 64-character fingerprints and are hex-validated, so nothing abbreviated or partial can address anyone. An ambiguous name — two live actors sharing one name — resolves to nobody rather than to a guess, on both sides.\n\nReply integrity likewise. A reply handle must name an existing frame in the resolved conversation: a parent from another conversation, or a sequence past the end, is refused rather than coerced. The parent's author is then added to the recipient set, which is what makes an exact reply reach the person replied to without widening delivery to the whole conversation.\n\nCapacity failure is atomic, which is the property I most wanted to see and the easiest to get wrong. Both canPublish and canEnqueue run before h.publish, and canEnqueue refuses if any single recipient inbox is full. So a capacity failure delivers to nobody and publishes nothing, rather than publishing and then failing partway through a delivery loop. addInbox is idempotent on top of that, so a retry cannot double-enqueue.\n\nThe retention split is real and correctly drawn. Every live recipient session is enrolled as a conversation participant; only inbox-capable sessions are enqueued. An older client therefore still sees the message through the conversation stream instead of the private inbox, which is what makes this landable without a flag day, and a recipient with no live session gets nothing — correct for an ephemeral surface, and consistent with the presence lease model rather than a silent loss.\n\nGates: internal/nexus, internal/service and internal/statusview pass; nexus and service pass under -race, which is where the concurrency claims live; go vet silent; go build ./... clean; UI tests 75 of 75; make ui-check reproduces the committed embed exactly; git diff --check clean; head is zero behind main.\n\nArchitecture: internal/nexus, internal/service, internal/statusview and ui. The kernel and the workroom fold are untouched, and the nexus stays application-neutral — Message carries opaque recipients and text, and the roster judgement lives in the service. The layer contract is preserved, so docs/reference/architecture.md does not move.\n\nSecurity: no widening I could find. The three things that would matter — publishing as another actor, addressing someone not named, and losing an accepted frame — are each refused by an explicit check rather than by construction, which is the right way round because a check can be tested and I tested them. The bounds are all present: message text, ID, recipient count, payload size and pending inbox depth.\n\nSimplification: I looked and found nothing worth cutting. The two implementations of the boundary rule are the one duplication, and they are unavoidable — the browser has to decide what to underline before it can ask the server anything. They agree today; a shared fixture would be the way to keep them agreeing, and I would not block on it.\n\nOne non-blocking finding, outside the conditions you set. The Go side excludes retired and non-participant actors when resolving an address; the TypeScript side resolves against the unfiltered roster. Their purposes differ — the server is choosing ephemeral recipients, the browser is filling body.mentions on a durable statement — so this is not a delivery divergence and nothing is delivered to a retired actor. But it does mean a durable statement can record a mention of an actor who holds no roles and may not be addressed, which is a claim the roster would not support. Worth a look when someone is next in that file; not a reason to hold this head.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:30206869d55828c9a4eb7d3c16d3cb71fe0cac8d","head":"c0c70300381730f3952db3c0a837c2beeb04121c","verdict":"approved"}}